@@ -41,3 +41,4 @@ a later package, that a reviewer may want to change.
 | GR27 | **Page result for a changed release adoption (P2-2a f2).** The "changed after my Wi-Fi page showed it" notice (P2-2a f1) ends "Make the request again if still needed." for every page-confirmed item the owner asked for. A release adoption the pipeline proposed (`meta.change.*` with Origin `change`) is page-confirmed too, but the owner made no request, so its notice ends "Nothing is needed." and promises no new offer (CH-12). The gate passes `change.Decided` `declined=false` for this close (the reason is not "owner"), so the proposal drops with no decline recorded, but the update check (UPD-5, `maintain` Loop 3) keeps the version as proposed and offers it again only after a restart or a newer release (L3 on #363; wiring the drop back to Loop 3 is a release row). The origin test reads the journal intent, as the gate already does for `Decided`, and names the pipeline origin as the string `"change"` because the gate does not import `change` (ARC-2). | CH-12, UPD-5 | Once a dropped proposal is offered again, or the owner can trigger an update check, say so in the notice. |
 | GR28 | **Follow wiring (OSS-10w2; security 323-1, OSS-10w L3).** `FollowID` and the gate admit only a lower-case hex nonce of 1 to `MaxFollowNonce` (64) characters, so no `/` can shift the ID's parse; agentosd's page path draws 16 random bytes per request. A named follow may not start with `ReservedFollowName` ("the AgentOS project", any case), so its line can never read as switching back. Both kinds of request carry `Detail` "root fingerprint <FollowPrint>": the digest's first 8 hex characters as two groups of 4, so the card never carries a run of digits that reads as a 6-digit code however the digest falls; the page shows the same print first. The card does not carry the root's key count (323-1 asked for it): the intent ID names only the digest, and carrying a count would put an unchecked number in the ID; the page shows the keys. 8 hex characters are 32 bits, which a fork could grind to match another root's print: the print tells two honest roots apart, and the name, the reserved prefix and WF1 at Execute carry the security. `FollowNameOK` lets agentosd say why a name was not asked. | OSS-10, CH-3, CH-10 | A longer print, or the key count in the ID, if the card is ever the only place the owner checks the root. |
 | GR29 | **Per-grant resume on the box's Wi-Fi page (W5a-resume; Security R2 on #169).** A paused grant resumes only through the page: `Paused` lists each paused grant with what resuming lets run (`Describe`), who paused it ("you", "Loop 2") and the pause's intent ID, which a grant now records (`Pause`, `PausedBy`). `AskResume` submits a `resume` grant from origin `local` naming that pause (`Spec.Pause`); evaluate denies a resume without a pause or from any other origin (an owner text, a guest, Loop 2). The item's Detail carries the grant's description and the pause ID, so the page code is bound to that grant and pause through the item's sum (D1 on #165: another request's sum is refused), each code is spent across page and texts (D2: a reused code is refused), and an empty code is refused. Apply re-checks the pause, so a resume asked before a later pause cannot end it. A resume recorded before this package names no pause and still replays. A second ask for the same open pause returns the open request. Asks are paced with the gate's batch (CH-15), so one reaches Approvals after the batch window. The owner channel texts the page's answers (owner.LocalAnswer). | CH-3, CH-10, OP-5, LOOP-9 | A resume by text would need its own code binding; dropping `Spec.Pause` would let a stale ask end a later pause. |
+| GR30 | **Refusals carry fixed guest text (SR2-3j).** Every refusal from `Gate.Check` is a `refusal`, the one grants type on guesterr's allowlist: owner text (which may name paths, IDs, the owner's own words or a parse error) and a `guesterr.Literal` for the guest. A denial the owner did not give names its cause: expired, void (wrong codes) and restart each have their own text, not "not approved by the owner" (lens on #396). The owner's decline reason, a stale approval's timing, a failed attempts read and a failed owner channel reach the guest only as fixed text; `annotate` fills `GuestReason` from `Reason` only where `Reason` is itself fixed. Every rig test checks that each denial has guest text and none names a path or the canary. |
